---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import ShiftCalculator from '../components/ShiftCalculator.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'When should I start arranging a Christmas swap?',
    a: 'The moment you can see the conflict — which this page gives you up to 12 months out. In most 24/7 workplaces the informal rule is first-asked, first-granted, and the colleagues most willing to work December 25 (in exchange for New Year\'s Eve, or for a summer weekend) get claimed by early fall.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do night shifts on December 24 count as working Christmas?',
    a: 'On the calendar here, a shift belongs to the day it starts. A night shift starting December 24 runs into Christmas morning, and a night shift starting December 25 means Christmas dinner is your pre-work meal. Families usually care about different halves of the day — check which one your rotation actually takes before burning a swap on it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Christmas premium pay required by law?',
    a: 'No US federal law requires extra pay for working Christmas; the FLSA treats it as an ordinary workday. Holiday premiums come from your employer\'s policy or a union contract — commonly time-and-a-half or double time. Check yours, then use the shift differential calculator to see what the day is actually worth.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which rotations are luckiest for Christmas?',
    a: 'None, over time — that is the honest answer. Every common rotation (2-2-3, Pitman, DuPont, 4-on-4-off, 48/96, Kelly) drifts against the calendar year, so crews take turns. What you can control is knowing early which year is yours.',
  },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="Am I Working on Christmas? — Check Your Shift Rotation Instantly"
  description="Find out in seconds whether your rotating shift schedule has you working Christmas Day — this year and next. Works for 2-2-3, Pitman, DuPont, 4-on-4-off, 48/96, and Kelly rotations."
  h1="Am I working on Christmas?"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>
    Every shift worker's December question, answered in ten seconds. Pick your rotation, enter the
    first day of any work stretch you know, and the list below tells you whether
    <strong>December 25 is a duty day for your crew</strong> — along with every other US federal
    holiday in the next 12 months, so you can see the whole holiday season in one look.
  </p>

  <ShiftCalculator defaultPattern="all" highlightHoliday="Christmas Day" />

  <h2>Why the answer changes every year</h2>
  <p>
    Rotating schedules run on cycles of 6, 8, 9, 14, or 28 days — none of which divides into 365. So
    your rotation drifts against the calendar, and the crew that worked Christmas last year is
    usually somewhere else in the cycle this year. On a <a href="/2-2-3-panama-schedule-calculator">
    2-2-3</a>, the 14-day cycle moves by one day each year (two after a leap year); on a
    <a href="/48-96-shift-calendar">48/96</a>, the six-day cycle lands wherever the drift takes it.
    Nobody can eyeball this. One anchor date and a calculator can.
  </p>

  <h2>If the answer is "working" — your options, ranked</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Swap early.</strong> The trade market for December 25 clears months ahead; colleagues
    without small children often prefer the quiet holiday shift plus a returned favor in July.
    <strong>Split the day.</strong> If you are on nights, the shift starting December 25 still
    leaves the morning for presents — many families simply move dinner. <strong>Claim the premium.
    </strong> If your contract pays double time, a worked Christmas plus an off New Year's may beat
    the reverse; run your actual numbers in the
    <a href="/shift-differential-calculator">shift differential calculator</a>.
    <strong>Aim leave at the cycle.</strong> One well-placed vacation day next to your rotation's
    natural break can buy a five-day Christmas without touching a swap.
  </p>

  <h2>Working the season, not just the day</h2>
  <p>
    Generate your calendar once and look at the whole stretch from Thanksgiving to New Year's. The
    common heartbreak is not Christmas itself but discovering that your rotation takes Christmas
    <em>and</em> New Year's in the same year — which is exactly the case where an early trade fixes
    both. Export the year to your phone calendar and let your family plan around the facts instead
    of your best guess. Also check <a href="/am-i-working-on-thanksgiving">Thanksgiving</a> while
    you are here.
  </p>
</ToolPage>
